-- title:
--   Proposition 3.10: Pointed orbit hulls in reductive Lie algebras
-- statement:
--   All vector spaces and Lie algebras are finite dimensional and real, with their usual Hausdorff topology. An invariant set is invariant under the group generated by $e^{\operatorname{ad}y}$. A closed convex set is pointed when its recession edge is zero. Write $\mathcal O_x=\operatorname{Inn}(\mathfrak g)x$, $\operatorname{co}(x)=\overline{\operatorname{conv}}\mathcal O_x$, and $C_x=\operatorname{cone}(\mathcal O_x)$. Admissibility means existence of a pointed generating invariant closed convex subset.
--
--   Let $\mathfrak g=\mathfrak g_{\rm compact}\oplus\bigoplus_{j=1}^k\mathfrak g_j$ be reductive, where the first factor is the maximal compact ideal (including the center) and each remaining factor is simple noncompact. For hermitian factors choose a maximal proper invariant cone $W_j$, and for other factors set $W_j=\{0\}$. Under the direct-product identification,
--
--   $$\mathfrak g_{co}=\mathfrak g_{\rm compact}\times\prod_{j=1}^k(W_j\cup(-W_j)).$$
--
--   The Lean statement uses the bijective tuple-sum map for this identification; no ambient admissibility is required, and $k=0$ is included.
-- source:
--   Elements in pointed invariant cones in Lie algebras and corresponding affine pairs, arXiv:2110.07297v1, PDF p. 10, Proposition 3.10: Pointed orbit hulls in reductive Lie algebras

import Definitions.Def_QAlgorithms_Papers_Independent_Frontier30_P26

